IN ASSEMBLY FILES:


    Comment at the start with title, claimed_effect (optional), author

    Commennt in between each line of code also sometimes explining what each line
of assembly is from the finished line of code.
Ex :
    /*
      soc_des = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, IPPROTO_UDP);
     */

    mov %r0, $2     /* AF_INET */
    mov %r1, $2     /* SOCK_DGRAM */
    mov %r2, $17        /* IPPRTOTO_UDP */
    push {%r0, %r1, %r2}
    mov %r0, $1     /* socket */
    mov %r1, %sp
    svc 0x00900066
    add %sp, %sp, $12

    mov %r6, %r0        /* r6 = soc_des */

    Varations:

IN C FILES:

    Large comment at the start with title, date, arhitecture, website, e-mail,
author, hosts and a DISSASEMBLY of the .text

    After that comes the C code, libs at the top, the shelcode is in a char*
made entirly from hex. Some line have comment at the end with explinations.
Lastly there is the main call where we fprintf the shellcode.

    Varations:
        There can be multiple versions based on how many nytes you want it on,
    the other versions are in comment above the standard one.


WARNING:
    The comments are not written in the same format
    Some start with /* ... */ and // others use ;
    Comments with ; are also sorounded by ----- lines top and bottom
    Not everything has dissademblies, its actually rare. The general dinamic is
saying what the shellcode does at the start in some comment, then the instruction
can be sometimes found before assembly code representing what line if code the
assembly is equivalent to.
    There are also some cases where there is not a lot of data. One comment at the
start with a bit of info and thats it.
